From 24cc2cc8699dd5b4792264728ea484daeac82356 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kevin Rajan <7121943+kvnloo@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 04:09:59 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] feat(anthropic): opt-in server-side context editing
 (clear_tool_uses)

#526 asks for Anthropic's context editing API on Claude routes. #1147 only
changed OAuth token and header plumbing, and main still sends no
context_management on the anthropic_messages path, although the computer-use
docs already describe it as active.

compression.anthropic_context_editing (default false) adds
context_management.edits = [clear_tool_uses_20250919] and the
context-management-2025-06-27 beta to main-turn requests, for Claude models
on the native Anthropic API only. The gate follows native compaction: it is
re-checked per request and stays off when compression is disabled or
checkpoint_required is set. The trigger sits 8,192 tokens below the local
compression trigger, and each clear must free at least 20% of it, so the
server goes first. Local compression stays enabled, but it decides on the
provider-reported prompt size, which after a real clear is the post-clear
size, so it fires later while the resent transcript keeps growing; that
regime is not tested against the real API. A structured 400 naming
context_management disables the feature for the session and retries once,
through the recovery rung native compaction already uses. Like
codex_responses_native, the key busts the gateway agent cache and
hot-reloads in TUI sessions. The computer-use page now names the key that
turns it on.

With the flag off, requests are unchanged. The feature manages the context
window and does not save money: each clear rewrites the cached prefix from
the first cleared result onward. clear_thinking is not sent.

Refs #526

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
